WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.500
An evidence matrix makes the logic behind a deployment decision visible.

2
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.900
Start with a requirement, identify the configured interface that must satisfy it,

3
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.580
and attach a record that actually supports the claim.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.760
Then state the review result and any remaining gap.

5
00:00:14.241 --> 00:00:18.000
The animation follows a product claim through the governing document, the proposed

6
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.160
configuration and qualification evidence.

7
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.500
Notice the distinction between documented and demonstrated.

8
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.380
A drawing can specify an intended relationship.

9
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.400
Inspection or test can show that a supplied assembly meets an acceptance

10
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:30.820
condition.

11
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.520
Neither should be labeled complete merely because the other exists.

12
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:39.080
For an unresolved item, record its consequence, owner and closure condition.

13
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.940
A gap in service access may block installation; missing fault-case capacity evidence

14
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.660
may block full-load operation.

15
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.540
Conditional progress must name the permitted activity and restriction.

16
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.040
Keep the matrix concise enough to use in a real review, but

17
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.020
specific enough that another engineer can reconstruct the decision without relying on

18
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.320
the meeting participants' memory.

